ion is catalogued as CapSupport via Engine.ActionCapability, (c) builtinRoleCaps admits CapSupport for exactly RoleSupport via BuiltinRoles(). Each fires before the row walk so a regression that drifted the catalog or role matrix gives a fast targeted failure at the seam, NOT a confusing per-row mismatch many lines later. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed set is constructed from registered enumerations the engine looks up by name — assert exhaustiveness in BOTH directions at the head of the test, then walk the table. (3) `REDACTION CANARY VIA SECRET MARKER IN PRINCIPAL ID, ASSERTED ON EVERY CONTENTION ITERATION` — adminEndpointPrincipal seeds the literal `ADMINENDPOINTSECRETMARKER` into every Principal ID, and the contention burst's per-iteration check calls reasonContainsSubstring(got.Reason, marker) to assert the Reason never echoes principal-supplied data. The Reason set is closed today so the check is structurally redundant, but it catches a future engine change that widened Reason to caller-supplied data on its FIRST iteration, NOT after a real credential leaks into a log. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint produces a structured outcome (Reason, Code, etc.) and is fed caller-supplied identifiers — seed a marker in the caller-supplied input and scan the output on every burst iteration; the marker is the chokepoint between "decides via" and "echoes the input". Two engine gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) Principal struct uses `Disabled bool` (default false = enabled), NOT an `Enabled` field — invented `Enabled:true` is a compile error; the zero-value-is-enabled invariant matches the existing principalIn helper. (B) Grant-only principals (Role="" + non-empty Grants) skip the unknown-role guard at engine.go:109 because the guard is conditional on `principal.Role != ""`; leave Role unset rather than setting a sentinel string the engine would reject. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF